  "generated_text": "To find the least positive multiple of 72 that has exactly 16 positive factors, we start by analyzing the prime factorization of 72 and then determine the form of the number that meets the factor count requirement.\n\nFirst, let's factorize 72:\n\\[\n72 = 2^3 \\times 3^2\n\\]\n\nWe need a number \\( N \\) that is a multiple of 72 and has exactly 16 positive factors. If \\( N \\) has the prime factorization \\( N = 2^a \\times 3^b \\times k \\), where \\( k \\) is an integer not divisible by 2 or 3, then the total number of factors of \\( N \\) is given by:\n\\[\n(a+1)(b+1)(\\text{factors of } k)\n\\]\n\nGiven that \\( N \\) must have exactly 16 factors, we set up the equation:\n\\[\n(a+1)(b+1)(\\text{factors of } k) = 16\n\\]\n\nWe need to find factor combinations of 16:\n\\[\n16 = 16 \\times 1 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n16 = 8 \\times 2 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n16 = 4 \\times 4 \\times 1\n\\]\n\\[\n16 = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2\n\\]\n\\[\n16 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 4\n\\]\n\nNext, we consider the constraints on \\( a \\) and \\( b \\) from the factorization of 72:\n\\[\na \\geq 3 \\quad (\\text{since } 72 = 2^3 \\times 3^2)\n\\]\n\\[\nb \\geq 2 \\quad (\\text{since } 72 = 2^3 \\times 3^2)\n\\]\n\nWe will test each combination of factorizations to find the smallest \\( N \\).\n\n1. **Combination: \\( 16 = 16 \\times 1 \\times 1 \\)**\n   \\[\n   a+1 = 16 \\implies a = 15\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b+1 = 1 \\implies b = 0\n   \\]\n   This does not fit since \\( b \\geq 2 \\).\n\n2. **Combination: \\( 16 = 8 \\times 2 \\times 1 \\)**\n   \\[\n   a+1 = 8 \\implies a = 7\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b+1 = 2 \\implies b = 1\n   \\]\n   This does not fit since \\( b \\geq 2 \\).\n\n3. **Combination: \\( 16 = 4 \\times 4 \\times 1 \\)**\n   \\[\n   a+1 = 4 \\implies a = 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b+1 = 4 \\implies b = 3\n   \\]\n   This fits, but we need to check if \\( k \\) can be a product of primes greater than 3.\n\n4. **Combination: \\( 16 = 4 \\times 2 \\times 2 \\)**\n   \\[\n   a+1 = 4 \\implies a = 3\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b+1 = 2 \\implies b = 1\n   \\]\n   This does not fit since \\( b \\geq 2 \\).\n\n5. **Combination: \\( 16 = 2 \\times 2 \\times 4 \\)**\n   \\[\n   a+1 = 2 \\implies a = 1\n   \\]\n   \\[\n   b+1 = 2 \\implies b = 1\n   \\]\n   This does not fit since \\( a \\geq 3 \\) and \\( b \\geq 2 \\).\n\nConsidering the valid combinations, the only feasible one is \\( a = 3 \\) and \\( b = 3 \\). Thus, \\( N \\) must be of the form:\n\\[\nN = 2^3 \\times 3^3 \\times k\n\\]\n\nTo minimize \\( N \\), we choose \\( k = 1 \\):\n\\[\nN = 2^3 \\times 3^3 = 8 \\times 27 = 216\n\\]\n\nHowever, 216 is not a multiple of 72. We need to include the factor 72 in \\( N \\):\n\\[\nN = 2^3 \\times 3^3 \\times 2^3 \\times 3^2 = 2^6 \\",
